"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative findings included a high baseline PSA of 7. The surgical pathology revealed a Gleason 4+3 (ISUP grade group 3), positive surgical margins, and invasion of the seminal vesicles, which are adverse features. The absence of lymph nodes removed is noted, and the biopsy showed a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2). The lack of documented nodal sampling introduces uncertainty, as pNx does not equate to pN0.\n\nCountervailing factors include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and the absence of lymph node removal. The primary uncertainty lies in the missing postoperative PSA and subsequent trajectory, which would significantly revise the prognosis. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their trend is crucial for further assessment. The current horizon suggests a follow-up period within the range of 21 to 49 months without an event. The fixed estimated horizon is 35.30 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 21.18–49.42 months; its coverage has not been validated."
